ng out” — not from a few hours of jogging a week, but from a heart that never gets a break. The saying is warning against that kind of life, and it’s right. 3. **Ignoring warning signs and pushing through.** The marathoner who collapses often had symptoms — chest tightness, unusual shortness of breath, a family history — but “powered through” because they thought a strong person doesn’t quit. That *is* overdoing it in the worst way: treating a cry for help as a challenge to overcome. The body’s signals were ignored, and disaster followed. ### Where the saying gets misapplied The confusion happens when people assume *any* vigorous use is the same as “overdoing it.” But an hour of moderate aerobic exercise a few times a week — where you can still talk, where your heart rate goes up and then fully recovers — is not the relentless, grinding overload the old saying warns about. It’s a recoverable stress that the body was built for. That’s not nitpicking; it’s the difference between watering a plant and drowning it, or between doing a few sets of squats and squatting a truck axle all day until your spine gives out. ### An analogy that doesn’t rely on physiology Think of your heart like a muscle — because it is one. Your biceps won’t wear out from lifting a dumbbell a few times a week; they’ll get stronger. But if you try to lift your maximum weight every single day without rest, or lift with a torn tendon, you’ll wreck it. The old saying “don’t overdo it” is warning you against the wreckage, not against the dumbbell. The heart follows the same logic: use it, rest it, don’t abuse it, and absolutely don’t strain it when it’s broken. ### So what’s the simple, non-technical takeaway? **“Don’t overdo it” is true. But “overdoing it” means either asking a sick heart to work too hard, or never giving a healthy heart a chance to recover, or ignoring that something’s wrong. It doesn’t mean that using your heart the way it evolved to be used — with intermittent, manageable effort followed by rest — will wear it out.** In fact, the heart that is never asked to work vigorously gradually becomes a heart that’s vulnerable to sudden strain precisely because it’s deconditioned. That’s why so many heart attacks happen during unaccustomed heavy exertion (like shoveling snow) in sedentary people, not in people who regularly train. I’m not trying to be clever or contrarian. I’m trying to protect the wisdom of “don’t overdo it” by applying it precisely where it fits, rather than letting it scare people away from the very thing that keeps their hearts resilient. Folk wisdom and modern medicine actually agree: the heart needs both work and rest, in the right measure, at the right time. That’s not nitpicking—that’s just knowing the line between “use” and “overuse.”
