ry lifestyle, poor diet, uncontrolled blood pressure, and smoking. A lifetime of moderate aerobic use flattens that slope. It doesn’t make the heart immortal, but it means that at age 80, the active person’s heart is functioning more like a 60-year-old’s heart—far less likely to fail from age-related stiffening, and far more likely to fail from some other final cause, like cancer or infection, while the heart is still strong enough to sustain life. When old people’s hearts “wear out,” you’re often seeing the cumulative damage of decades of silent disease—tiny insults from high blood pressure, high cholesterol, high blood sugar, inflammation. The heart that was used aerobically throughout life accumulated far fewer of those insults, so its eventual stop is postponed, and often it never becomes the limiting factor. In many very active elders, the heart is the last organ standing. ### Even with perfect use, the heart still stops. But that doesn’t mean the beats “wore it out.” Every living system has a biological clock. Telomeres shorten, DNA repair mechanisms slow, stem cell reserves dwindle. Eventually, even the best-maintained heart reaches a point of vulnerability. But calling that “wearing out from use” is like saying a 100-year-old person died because they “used up” all their breaths. The breaths weren’t the cause; aging was. The heart’s final beat is the event, but the cause was the relentless march of biology, not the mechanical tally of contractions. You’re worried that all those beats add up and eventually take their toll. But if that simple arithmetic were true, the healthiest hearts at 80 wouldn’t be the ones that pumped hard and often with exercise—which in fact lowers the total number of beats per day and strengthens the pump. And the sickest hearts wouldn’t so overwhelmingly belong to those who sat still, stressed, and let their vessels stiffen. The heart doesn’t die from being used; it dies when the biological system that sustains it runs out of the abil